---
import ContentLayout from "../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Watch Multiple Twitch Streams at Once | Stream Mix"
  description="Watch multiple Twitch streams and chats at once. Learn how to add channels, arrange the grid, and troubleshoot Twitch embeds in Stream Mix."
  heading="Watch multiple Twitch streams at once"
  platform="twitch"
>
  <p>
    Stream Mix lets you watch several Twitch channels and chats in one
    customizable browser workspace. You can keep an official tournament
    broadcast beside player perspectives, compare creators covering the same
    event, or follow several speedruns without managing a stack of tabs.
  </p>

  <h2>How to add multiple Twitch streams</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Copy a creator’s channel URL, such as
      <code>https://www.twitch.tv/twitch</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Paste the URL into the Stream Mix header. Twitch is selected automatically
      and the channel name is extracted for you.
    </li>
    <li>
      Choose Stream + Chat, Stream, or Chat depending on what you want the
      window to contain.
    </li>
    <li>Select Add, then repeat the process for every Twitch channel.</li>
    <li>
      Drag windows by their title bars and resize them from either lower corner.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    You can also select Twitch manually and enter only the channel name. Both
    <code>twitch</code> and <code>twitch.tv/twitch</code> identify the same channel.
    Stream Mix is designed for live channel pages; Twitch clips and video-on-demand
    URLs are not currently supported.
  </p>

  <h2>Choose the right Twitch display mode</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Stream + Chat</strong> loads Twitch’s combined experience so you can
      watch and participate in the same window.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stream</strong> keeps the window focused on video and works well for
      secondary perspectives where you do not need chat.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Chat</strong> is useful when the main broadcast is already open elsewhere
      or when you want several community conversations beside one primary stream.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A practical tournament layout is one large Stream + Chat window for the
    official broadcast, with smaller Stream windows for player perspectives. If
    the workspace becomes crowded, minimize channels you want to keep for later
    or enable Compact mode to reclaim the space used by site and window headers.
  </p>

  <h2>How Twitch embeds work</h2>
  <p>
    Stream Mix uses Twitch’s official player and chat embeds rather than
    restreaming the video. Twitch requires embedded experiences to know the
    domain that contains them, so Stream Mix supplies the current hostname
    automatically. This is why production embeds use
    <code>streammix.app</code> while local testing uses
    <code>localhost</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Account actions and chat can still depend on Twitch authentication, browser
    cookie settings, privacy extensions, and Twitch service availability. The
    embedded player also needs enough room to display its controls, so a very
    small window may be difficult to use. See Twitch’s
    <a href="https://dev.twitch.tv/docs/embed/" rel="external"
      >official embed requirements</a
    >
    for the platform-level rules.
  </p>

  <h2>Troubleshoot a Twitch stream or chat</h2>
  <h3>The URL is not accepted</h3>
  <p>
    Use the channel’s main URL rather than a clip, category, search, or VOD
    page. If necessary, select Twitch and enter the channel name shown after
    <code>twitch.tv/</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>The channel is offline</h3>
  <p>
    Twitch’s player will show the channel’s offline experience instead of live
    video. The window is still valid and can remain in your saved layout for the
    next broadcast.
  </p>

  <h3>Video works but chat or account controls do not</h3>
  <p>
    Check whether the same channel works directly on Twitch, then temporarily
    disable strict tracking protection or content-blocking extensions for Stream
    Mix. Some authenticated Twitch features require cookies that a
    privacy-focused browser may block inside an iframe.
  </p>

  <h3>Several streams stutter</h3>
  <p>
    Every active player decodes its own video and uses additional bandwidth.
    Lower the quality of secondary channels inside the Twitch player, mute audio
    you do not need, use Chat-only windows where appropriate, or minimize
    streams until you need them again.
  </p>

  <h2>Mix Twitch with other platforms</h2>
  <p>
    A workspace can combine Twitch with
    <a href="/guides/watch-multiple-youtube-streams"
      >multiple YouTube live streams</a
    >
    and <a href="/guides/watch-multiple-kick-streams">multiple Kick streams</a>.
    The complete <a href="/guides/watch-multiple-streams"
      >multistream viewing guide</a
    >
    explains layout sharing, Compact mode, and mixed-platform workflows. For broader
    playback and privacy answers, visit the <a href="/faq">FAQ</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="/" class="btn btn-primary">Open the Twitch multistream viewer</a>
  </p>
</ContentLayout>
